Let's take a moment to recognize

00:20:00.011 --> 00:20:01.708
our families and friends.

00:20:01.708 --> 00:20:11.708
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:20:24.618 --> 00:20:27.593
&gt;&gt; Finally, we have more than 500

00:20:27.593 --> 00:20:30.466
students from the MBA, MSc, and

00:20:30.466 --> 00:20:31.810
PhD programs.

00:20:31.810 --> 00:20:32.924
So, let's hear it for

00:20:32.924 --> 00:20:34.281
our graduating students.

00:20:34.281 --> 00:20:44.281
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:20:48.554 --> 00:20:50.666
&gt;&gt; My opening remarks today

00:20:50.666 --> 00:20:53.180
are about what it means to live

00:20:53.180 --> 00:20:55.705
a good life, I'll be brief.

00:20:55.705 --> 00:20:59.491
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; I want to start

00:20:59.491 --> 00:21:02.450
however with your experience

00:21:02.450 --> 00:21:03.639
at the GSB.

00:21:03.639 --> 00:21:05.906
When you arrived we told you that

00:21:05.906 --> 00:21:08.600
Stanford would widen your aperture.

00:21:08.600 --> 00:21:10.539
You would meet new people,

00:21:10.539 --> 00:21:12.817
you would encounter new ideas,

00:21:12.817 --> 00:21:15.179
you would have new experiences,

00:21:15.179 --> 00:21:17.130
you've made that happen.

00:21:17.130 --> 00:21:18.617
You tackled finance and

00:21:18.617 --> 00:21:19.961
accounting some of-

00:21:19.961 --> 00:21:27.019
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; You were asked

00:21:27.019 --> 00:21:29.285
to think hard about leadership and

00:21:29.285 --> 00:21:31.482
about shareholder capitalism.

00:21:31.482 --> 00:21:34.175
Some of you did original research.

00:21:34.175 --> 00:21:40.015
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; Some

00:21:40.015 --> 00:21:42.237
of you were ridiculous costumes and

00:21:42.237 --> 00:21:44.318
flew to Las Vegas for 24 hours.

00:21:44.318 --> 00:21:50.847
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; All of us

00:21:50.847 --> 00:21:52.849
had a new experience this winter,

00:21:52.849 --> 00:21:54.780
when we stopped using electronic

00:21:54.780 --> 00:21:56.166
devices during class.

00:21:56.166 --> 00:22:02.452
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; Let me amend that,

00:22:02.452 --> 00:22:04.047
when we tried to stop using

00:22:04.047 --> 00:22:06.080
electronic devices during class.

00:22:06.080 --> 00:22:09.418
[APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; This spring, some of

00:22:09.418 --> 00:22:10.992
you took what you learned and

00:22:10.992 --> 00:22:12.378
started new companies.

00:22:12.378 --> 00:22:13.677
Some of you danced in front of a

00:22:13.677 --> 00:22:15.188
a thousand people in the GSB show.

00:22:15.188 --> 00:22:19.394
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Apparently,

00:22:19.394 --> 00:22:20.926
some very funny things happened

00:22:20.926 --> 00:22:22.287
this year at the school, but

00:22:22.287 --> 00:22:23.365
I couldn't follow any

00:22:23.365 --> 00:22:25.022
of the jokes in this year's show.

00:22:25.022 --> 00:22:28.269
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; A few weeks ago,

00:22:28.269 --> 00:22:29.702
many of us gathered in

00:22:29.702 --> 00:22:32.173
Bing Concert Hall for something I'd

00:22:32.173 --> 00:22:33.847
never seen at Stanford,

00:22:33.847 --> 00:22:36.236
the performance of classical music

00:22:36.236 --> 00:22:37.909
and dance that showcased

00:22:37.909 --> 00:22:40.238
extraordinary student talents.

00:22:40.238 --> 00:22:42.778
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Most importantly,

00:22:42.778 --> 00:22:43.424
every day,

00:22:43.424 --> 00:22:44.912
you came together with your

00:22:44.912 --> 00:22:46.859
classmates from around the world.

00:22:46.859 --> 00:22:49.186
You push yourselves to expand your

00:22:49.186 --> 00:22:51.197
thinking and try new things.

00:22:51.197 --> 00:22:53.764
And one of my great wishes for

00:22:53.764 --> 00:22:56.549
you is to keep doing just that.

00:22:56.549 --> 00:22:58.819
At a faculty meeting earlier this

00:22:58.819 --> 00:23:01.009
year, my colleague Brian Lowry

00:23:01.009 --> 00:23:03.200
handed me a paper by a psychology

00:23:03.200 --> 00:23:05.309
professor at the University of

00:23:05.309 --> 00:23:07.757
Chicago, named Shigehiro Oishi.

00:23:07.757 --> 00:23:10.696
Oishi's studies what it means to

00:23:10.696 --> 00:23:12.276
live a good life.

00:23:12.276 --> 00:23:15.232
He is not the first, Socrates and

00:23:15.232 --> 00:23:18.295
Aristotle got there long ago, but

00:23:18.295 --> 00:23:21.580
Oishi has a substantively new idea.

00:23:21.580 --> 00:23:24.028
They're two conceptions of the good

00:23:24.028 --> 00:23:26.319
life that have been much debated in

00:23:26.319 --> 00:23:28.296
philosophy and psychology.

00:23:28.296 --> 00:23:29.977
The first is happiness,

00:23:29.977 --> 00:23:31.977
the idea that we are fulfilled

00:23:31.977 --> 00:23:34.542
through friendships and experiences

00:23:34.542 --> 00:23:36.555
that bring joy and pleasure.

00:23:36.555 --> 00:23:39.178
The second is meaning, namely, that

00:23:39.178 --> 00:23:41.310
we should seek to have a purpose

00:23:41.310 --> 00:23:43.283
and commit ourselves to it.

00:23:43.283 --> 00:23:45.572
Aristotle called this the life of

00:23:45.572 --> 00:23:46.231
virtue.

00:23:46.231 --> 00:23:49.101
Oishi observes that when people

00:23:49.101 --> 00:23:51.759
reflect back on their lives,

00:23:51.759 --> 00:23:54.734
what they regret is generally not

00:23:54.734 --> 00:23:57.923
things they've done or failed at,

00:23:57.923 --> 00:24:00.809
but things they haven't done.

00:24:00.809 --> 00:24:03.236
He argues that this tells us

00:24:03.236 --> 00:24:05.883
something about a good life.

00:24:05.883 --> 00:24:08.247
Living a good life means, that we

00:24:08.247 --> 00:24:10.530
keep seeking new experiences,

00:24:10.530 --> 00:24:12.896
keep finding new perspectives,

00:24:12.896 --> 00:24:14.924
keep discovering new ways,

00:24:14.924 --> 00:24:16.794
to think about the world.

00:24:16.794 --> 00:24:18.917
These experiences needn't bring

00:24:18.917 --> 00:24:20.826
happiness, or even purpose.

00:24:20.826 --> 00:24:23.564
It's a third, and distinct concept.

00:24:23.564 --> 00:24:26.917
Oishi calls this a psychologically

00:24:26.917 --> 00:24:28.040
rich life.

00:24:28.040 --> 00:24:31.210
This is the life you've lived at

00:24:31.210 --> 00:24:32.388
Stanford.

00:24:32.388 --> 00:24:34.927
But in fact, I would argue that

00:24:34.927 --> 00:24:37.467
the GSB has prepared you for all

00:24:37.467 --> 00:24:40.212
three dimensions of a good life.

00:24:40.212 --> 00:24:42.358
You've made friendships that will

00:24:42.358 --> 00:24:44.147
last decades and be a source of

00:24:44.147 --> 00:24:46.304
great happiness and fulfillment.

00:24:46.304 --> 00:24:48.723
You've gained the skills and

00:24:48.723 --> 00:24:51.239
aspirations to pursue a life of

00:24:51.239 --> 00:24:53.088
purpose and meaning.

00:24:53.088 --> 00:24:54.847
And you've set a foundation to

00:24:54.847 --> 00:24:56.269
continue to broaden your

00:24:56.269 --> 00:24:58.436
perspective, to take risks, to live

00:24:58.436 --> 00:25:00.212
a psychologically rich life.

00:25:02.616 --> 00:25:03.116
I'm excited to watch your lives

00:25:03.116 --> 00:25:03.616
unfold in each of these ways.

00:25:08.959 --> 00:25:12.821
Now, I want to close with a quote

00:25:12.821 --> 00:25:15.502
from the GSB campus.

00:25:15.502 --> 00:25:16.867
If you walk across

00:25:16.867 --> 00:25:19.329
the Knight Management Center,

00:25:19.329 --> 00:25:21.970
you come upon a set of footprints,

00:25:21.970 --> 00:25:24.442
in the pavement of Town Square.

00:25:24.442 --> 00:25:26.514
They're the footprints of

00:25:26.514 --> 00:25:27.596
Phil Knight,

00:25:27.596 --> 00:25:29.847
who sat in one of these seats at

00:25:29.847 --> 00:25:32.744
a GSB graduation some 60 years ago.

00:25:32.744 --> 00:25:35.489
Next to the footprints is a quote

00:25:35.489 --> 00:25:36.513
from Phil.

00:25:36.513 --> 00:25:39.662
And it says, there comes a time in

00:25:39.662 --> 00:25:42.700
every life when the past recedes

00:25:42.700 --> 00:25:44.848
and the future opens.

00:25:44.848 --> 00:25:47.660
It's a moment when you turn to face

00:25:47.660 --> 00:25:48.830
the unknown.

00:25:48.830 --> 00:25:51.084
Some will turn back to what they

00:25:51.084 --> 00:25:53.253
already know, some will walk

00:25:53.253 --> 00:25:55.867
straight ahead into uncertainty.

00:25:55.867 --> 00:25:58.833
I can't tell you which one is

00:25:58.833 --> 00:26:02.418
right, but I can tell you which one

00:26:02.418 --> 00:26:03.790
is more fun.

00:26:03.790 --> 00:26:06.687
Stanford GSB Class of 2023,

00:26:06.687 --> 00:26:09.892
I wish you happiness, meaning, and

00:26:09.892 --> 00:26:13.100
continued adventure, a good life,

00:26:13.100 --> 00:26:14.869
congratulations.

00:26:14.869 --> 00:26:24.869
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:26:30.929 --> 00:26:33.025
&gt;&gt; Today's graduation speaker,

00:26:33.025 --> 00:26:35.352
Henry Fernandez, has been walking

00:26:35.352 --> 00:26:37.448
into uncertainty for decades,

00:26:37.448 --> 00:26:39.243
with purpose and success.

00:26:39.243 --> 00:26:41.417
Henry grew up in Nicaragua.

00:26:41.417 --> 00:26:43.800
After coming to the United States

00:26:43.800 --> 00:26:46.440
and considering a PhD in economics,

00:26:46.440 --> 00:26:47.885
he came to the GSB and

00:26:47.885 --> 00:26:49.347
completed his MBA.

00:26:49.347 --> 00:26:51.878
Today, Henry is the Chairman and

00:26:51.878 --> 00:26:52.874
CEO at MSCI,

00:26:52.874 --> 00:26:54.873
the global finance firm.

00:26:54.873 --> 00:26:57.132
He's been recognized multiple times

00:26:57.132 --> 00:26:59.117
is one of the world's best CEOs.

00:26:59.117 --> 00:27:02.716
In recent years, he's put MSCI at

00:27:02.716 --> 00:27:06.449
the forefront of climate finance.

00:27:06.449 --> 00:27:09.112
I especially admire Henry for his

00:27:09.112 --> 00:27:11.966
passionate support of GSB students

00:27:11.966 --> 00:27:14.628
and faculty over many years, for

00:27:14.628 --> 00:27:16.625
his original ideas, for

00:27:16.625 --> 00:27:19.193
his infectious energy, and for

00:27:19.193 --> 00:27:20.450
his big heart.

00:27:20.450 --> 00:27:21.820
Please join me in welcoming

00:27:21.820 --> 00:27:22.778
Henry Fernandez.

00:27:22.778 --> 00:27:32.778
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:27:37.923 --> 00:27:40.593
&gt;&gt; Well, good afternoon, everyone.

00:27:40.593 --> 00:27:43.431
I want to thank Dean Levin for

00:27:43.431 --> 00:27:45.921
his warm introduction.

00:27:45.921 --> 00:27:48.595
I would also like to welcome my

00:27:48.595 --> 00:27:51.377
fellow trustees of the Board of

00:27:51.377 --> 00:27:54.267
Stanford, faculty and staff,

00:27:54.267 --> 00:27:56.513
parents, relatives and

00:27:56.513 --> 00:27:58.771
distinguished guests.

00:27:58.771 --> 00:28:02.624
And last, but certainly not least,

00:28:02.624 --> 00:28:05.484
all graduating students,

00:28:05.484 --> 00:28:08.342
it is a tremendous honor and

00:28:08.342 --> 00:28:12.086
privilege for me to join you today.

00:28:14.530 --> 00:28:17.743
As you may have heard or read,

00:28:17.743 --> 00:28:21.468
I'm a proud member of the GSB class

00:28:21.468 --> 00:28:22.506
of 83.

00:28:22.506 --> 00:28:28.468
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; And

00:28:28.468 --> 00:28:32.026
just to confirm, even though I'm

00:28:32.026 --> 00:28:34.602
not a young man anymore,

00:28:34.602 --> 00:28:38.290
that was 1983, not 1883.

00:28:38.290 --> 00:28:42.874
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; 40 years ago,

00:28:42.874 --> 00:28:46.375
this very month, I was sitting

00:28:46.375 --> 00:28:49.751
exactly where you are today.

00:28:49.751 --> 00:28:52.188
I was pleased with my graduation,

00:28:53.521 --> 00:28:54.829
Excited, but

00:28:54.829 --> 00:28:58.099
anxious about my next chapter, and

00:28:58.099 --> 00:29:01.369
wondering what life would look like

00:29:01.369 --> 00:29:03.553
in the decades to come.

00:29:03.553 --> 00:29:07.172
I certainly have gained significant

00:29:07.172 --> 00:29:08.573
knowledge and

00:29:08.573 --> 00:29:10.914
experience at the GSB.

00:29:10.914 --> 00:29:14.902
But I surely wish I had learned

00:29:14.902 --> 00:29:19.368
much more of what I will share with

00:29:19.368 --> 00:29:20.974
you today.

00:29:20.974 --> 00:29:23.080
In my remarks,

00:29:23.080 --> 00:29:27.615
I would like to impart my lifelong

00:29:27.615 --> 00:29:31.666
learnings in several areas,

00:29:31.666 --> 00:29:36.526
namely, the miracle of optimism,

00:29:36.526 --> 00:29:40.906
confidence, and resilience.

00:29:40.906 --> 00:29:45.949
The surprising benefits of failure,

00:29:45.949 --> 00:29:50.203
the unexpected virtues of grunt

00:29:50.203 --> 00:29:54.143
work, &gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; And

00:29:54.143 --> 00:29:58.259
the magic of aligning skills with

00:29:58.259 --> 00:30:00.910
purpose and vision.

00:30:03.567 --> 00:30:05.100
But before I go through them,

00:30:05.100 --> 00:30:06.704
Just a quick and

00:30:06.704 --> 00:30:09.914
important word about long term

00:30:09.914 --> 00:30:12.264
perspective in life.

00:30:12.264 --> 00:30:16.512
Perspective is the perfect remedy

00:30:16.512 --> 00:30:20.908
for both irrational exuberance and

00:30:20.908 --> 00:30:22.821
on do despair.

00:30:22.821 --> 00:30:25.900
It is also essential to developing

00:30:25.900 --> 00:30:28.031
a vision of the future.

00:30:28.031 --> 00:30:32.356
We need long term perspective now

00:30:32.356 --> 00:30:34.522
more than ever.

00:30:34.522 --> 00:30:37.003
Watching the news on a year like

00:30:37.003 --> 00:30:38.626
this can make anyone

00:30:38.626 --> 00:30:41.591
feel pessimistic about the future.

00:30:41.591 --> 00:30:43.357
Take it from me,

00:30:43.357 --> 00:30:46.262
every GSB class thinks they

00:30:46.262 --> 00:30:49.294
are graduating at a uniquely

00:30:49.294 --> 00:30:52.596
difficult moment in history.

00:30:52.596 --> 00:30:57.902
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; In a way,

00:30:57.902 --> 00:31:00.714
they're all correct.

00:31:00.714 --> 00:31:03.704
As you go out into the world,

00:31:03.704 --> 00:31:07.315
you will face many challenges that

00:31:07.315 --> 00:31:10.804
did not exist in 1983, but at

00:31:10.804 --> 00:31:14.666
the same time, you will enjoy many,

00:31:14.666 --> 00:31:17.047
many opportunities.

00:31:17.047 --> 00:31:20.931
Geopolitically, 1983 represented

00:31:20.931 --> 00:31:23.928
a high point of Cold War tensions

00:31:23.928 --> 00:31:26.152
with the Soviet Union.

00:31:26.152 --> 00:31:29.641
Domestically, America's violent

00:31:29.641 --> 00:31:33.466
crime rate was 35% higher in 1983

00:31:33.466 --> 00:31:35.274
than in 2020.

00:31:35.274 --> 00:31:38.871
Economically, the month I

00:31:38.871 --> 00:31:43.721
graduated from the GSB, America's

00:31:43.721 --> 00:31:48.578
unemployment rate was over 10%.

00:31:48.578 --> 00:31:51.360
We were also emerging from

00:31:51.360 --> 00:31:54.017
the worst inflation from

00:31:54.017 --> 00:31:55.542
World War II.

00:31:55.542 --> 00:31:58.095
That same month,

00:31:58.095 --> 00:32:02.862
the Dow Jones Industrial Average

00:32:02.862 --> 00:32:07.629
never got above 1,250 which

00:32:07.629 --> 00:32:09.842
is as you know,

00:32:09.842 --> 00:32:14.792
less than 4% of its level today.

00:32:14.792 --> 00:32:17.431
My point here is not to diminish

00:32:17.431 --> 00:32:19.563
our current problems but

00:32:19.563 --> 00:32:22.404
rather to put them in a longer term

00:32:22.404 --> 00:32:23.736
perspective.

00:32:23.736 --> 00:32:26.094
It's snapshots in time,

00:32:26.094 --> 00:32:28.665
when not taken in context can

00:32:28.665 --> 00:32:32.095
be misleading and demoralizing, and

00:32:32.095 --> 00:32:35.434
they can perpetuate short termism.

00:32:39.460 --> 00:32:42.349
Thinking a longer view allows us

00:32:42.349 --> 00:32:45.024
to observe a wider arc of human

00:32:45.024 --> 00:32:46.201
progress and

00:32:46.201 --> 00:32:49.411
the tremendous opportunities that

00:32:49.411 --> 00:32:51.558
that progress brings.

00:32:54.282 --> 00:32:57.225
My own personal perspective was

00:32:57.225 --> 00:33:00.495
heavily shaped by the obstacles and

00:33:00.495 --> 00:33:03.656
struggles I endure in my late teens

00:33:03.656 --> 00:33:05.297
and my twenties.

00:33:05.297 --> 00:33:09.625
As you can tell from my accent,

00:33:09.625 --> 00:33:13.463
I grew up in the deep south.

00:33:13.463 --> 00:33:15.979
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; The very,

00:33:15.979 --> 00:33:21.143
very deep south of Central America.

00:33:21.143 --> 00:33:24.761
When I first came to America from

00:33:24.761 --> 00:33:27.443
Nicaragua at age 16,

00:33:27.443 --> 00:33:30.267
I did not speak English.

00:33:30.267 --> 00:33:31.915
Even as I learned,

00:33:31.915 --> 00:33:35.107
the language, many of my professors

00:33:35.107 --> 00:33:36.550
at Georgetown,

00:33:36.550 --> 00:33:38.823
could not understand me.

00:33:38.823 --> 00:33:41.641
At age 21, my home country

00:33:41.641 --> 00:33:44.342
was shaken by a very violent

00:33:44.342 --> 00:33:46.816
communist revolution.

00:33:46.816 --> 00:33:50.479
Suddenly, I found myself as

00:33:50.479 --> 00:33:54.763
a political refugee in America.

00:33:54.763 --> 00:33:57.271
Every six months, as the US

00:33:57.271 --> 00:34:00.219
government reviewed my case, I

00:34:00.219 --> 00:34:03.616
received a new deportation letter.

00:34:03.616 --> 00:34:05.940
When the second letter arrived, I

00:34:05.940 --> 00:34:08.344
told my lawyer, look, this is a big

00:34:08.344 --> 00:34:10.684
country let them come and find me.

00:34:10.684 --> 00:34:13.612
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; [LAUGH] After

00:34:13.612 --> 00:34:17.803
a while, when it became clear I

00:34:17.803 --> 00:34:22.479
could no longer return to my native

00:34:22.479 --> 00:34:27.168
Nicaragua, I felt totally lost.

00:34:27.168 --> 00:34:30.021
I had no answers to the most basic

00:34:30.021 --> 00:34:31.827
questions of life.

00:34:31.827 --> 00:34:33.802
Where would I live?

00:34:33.802 --> 00:34:36.132
What would I do?

00:34:36.132 --> 00:34:37.712
When I ever feel a true sense of

00:34:37.712 --> 00:34:39.684
identity I'm belonging anywhere.

00:34:39.684 --> 00:34:43.169
I was not able to conquer those

00:34:43.169 --> 00:34:46.942
demons until I got to Stanford.

00:34:48.683 --> 00:34:53.232
In that way, the GSB was a godsend,

00:34:53.232 --> 00:34:57.478
and helped me regain perspective

00:34:57.478 --> 00:35:01.126
and re-imagine my future.

00:35:01.126 --> 00:35:02.903
During my time here,

00:35:02.903 --> 00:35:05.480
I discover my first learning.

00:35:07.488 --> 00:35:11.449
The miracle of confidence,

00:35:11.449 --> 00:35:15.576
optimism, and resilience.

00:35:15.576 --> 00:35:19.390
I realize that the most successful

00:35:19.390 --> 00:35:21.890
people in life all have

00:35:21.890 --> 00:35:25.980
an unshakable belief in the future.

00:35:25.980 --> 00:35:28.931
And an unwavering confidence

00:35:28.931 --> 00:35:32.235
that they will find a path forward

00:35:32.235 --> 00:35:34.486
through that future.

00:35:34.486 --> 00:35:37.490
I never forgot during my first days

00:35:37.490 --> 00:35:40.289
on campus, watching a view from

00:35:40.289 --> 00:35:43.412
the top lecture by Nolan Bushnell.

00:35:43.412 --> 00:35:45.819
Bushnell has created more than 20

00:35:45.819 --> 00:35:48.389
companies including the video game,

00:35:48.389 --> 00:35:50.556
Atari and the entertainment and

00:35:50.556 --> 00:35:52.733
dining company Chuck E Cheese.

00:35:52.733 --> 00:35:57.499
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; When he came to

00:35:57.499 --> 00:36:01.058
Stanford 1981, he focus his speech

00:36:01.058 --> 00:36:03.367
on all his fail ventures.

00:36:05.049 --> 00:36:08.476
This seems strange to me.

00:36:08.476 --> 00:36:10.248
Even stranger was the fact that

00:36:10.248 --> 00:36:11.752
everyone applauded him.

00:36:14.139 --> 00:36:15.526
I asked myself,

00:36:15.526 --> 00:36:17.607
what was that all about?

00:36:17.607 --> 00:36:20.369
It took me a while to grasp that

00:36:20.369 --> 00:36:23.463
the students were not applauding

00:36:23.463 --> 00:36:26.448
failure, they were applauding

00:36:26.448 --> 00:36:28.005
risk-taking.

00:36:28.005 --> 00:36:31.397
Bushnell's point was the genuine

00:36:31.397 --> 00:36:34.439
innovation requires us to take

00:36:34.439 --> 00:36:38.066
risks and chances, and that failure

00:36:38.066 --> 00:36:41.235
can have surprising benefits.

00:36:43.205 --> 00:36:46.248
And believe me I have the scars to

00:36:46.248 --> 00:36:49.293
prove that since I embraced that

00:36:49.293 --> 00:36:51.339
throughout my life.

00:36:51.339 --> 00:36:53.160
Before MSCI,

00:36:53.160 --> 00:36:57.105
I failed in business not once,

00:36:57.105 --> 00:37:00.909
not twice, but three times.

00:37:00.909 --> 00:37:03.128
First, I tried to take over a

00:37:03.128 --> 00:37:05.998
savings bank in Buffalo, New York,

00:37:05.998 --> 00:37:08.971
very balmy weather like California.

00:37:08.971 --> 00:37:11.014
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; That failed.

00:37:11.014 --> 00:37:12.866
Then I established a private equity

00:37:12.866 --> 00:37:16.892
firm in Mexico, That failed.

00:37:16.892 --> 00:37:20.165
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; Then a GSB friend and

00:37:20.165 --> 00:37:23.268
I set up a media focused private

00:37:23.268 --> 00:37:25.534
equity firm in the US,

00:37:25.534 --> 00:37:27.336
that failed too.

00:37:27.336 --> 00:37:29.731
When I finally rejoined

00:37:29.731 --> 00:37:33.444
Morgan Stanley, where my career had

00:37:33.444 --> 00:37:36.574
started, I was almost broke.

00:37:36.574 --> 00:37:39.119
But still, I decided to take

00:37:39.119 --> 00:37:41.035
another big chance.

00:37:41.035 --> 00:37:42.219
At that time,

00:37:42.219 --> 00:37:44.883
MSCI was a failing joint venture

00:37:44.883 --> 00:37:47.154
between Morgan Stanley and

00:37:47.154 --> 00:37:48.833
the Capital Group,

00:37:48.833 --> 00:37:51.018
a large investment firm.

00:37:51.018 --> 00:37:53.867
Most people would not have consider

00:37:53.867 --> 00:37:56.527
this joint venture a good place to

00:37:56.527 --> 00:37:58.526
relaunch their career.

00:37:58.526 --> 00:37:59.486
And yet,

00:37:59.486 --> 00:38:02.965
over the past three decades we have

00:38:02.965 --> 00:38:06.670
succeeded beyond my wildest dreams.

00:38:06.670 --> 00:38:11.157
And we still believe that the best

00:38:11.157 --> 00:38:13.246
is yet to come.

00:38:13.246 --> 00:38:15.483
When you describe it that way,

00:38:15.483 --> 00:38:18.071
MSCI seems like a Hollywood movie.

00:38:19.730 --> 00:38:21.922
But I can assure you that it was

00:38:21.922 --> 00:38:24.557
filled with countless obstacles,

00:38:24.557 --> 00:38:26.849
uncertainties and setbacks.

00:38:29.340 --> 00:38:33.265
We only achieve success because

00:38:33.265 --> 00:38:37.775
of our willingness to risk failure.

00:38:40.582 --> 00:38:44.410
We only achieve success because of

00:38:44.410 --> 00:38:47.980
our willingness risk failure.

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:51.432
I have learned far,

00:38:51.432 --> 00:38:53.531
far more from my failures that I

00:38:53.531 --> 00:38:56.130
haven't learned from my successes.

00:38:57.680 --> 00:39:00.706
I have discovered the surprising

00:39:00.706 --> 00:39:02.760
benefits of failure,

00:39:02.760 --> 00:39:05.251
my second learning today.

00:39:07.470 --> 00:39:10.207
You would not be here today unless

00:39:10.207 --> 00:39:12.944
you had a strong internal drive to

00:39:12.944 --> 00:39:13.835
succeed.

00:39:13.835 --> 00:39:16.277
As you continue on your path,

00:39:16.277 --> 00:39:18.913
you should expect to do plenty of

00:39:18.913 --> 00:39:21.647
grunt work, no matter where your

00:39:21.647 --> 00:39:24.199
job is or what your title says.

00:39:25.830 --> 00:39:27.507
Indeed, grunt work is

00:39:27.507 --> 00:39:29.981
the foundation of all successful

00:39:29.981 --> 00:39:31.050
businesses.

00:39:32.380 --> 00:39:34.582
It is what teaches us

00:39:34.582 --> 00:39:37.958
the discipline of execution.

00:39:37.958 --> 00:39:39.114
Without it,

00:39:39.114 --> 00:39:42.266
even the most brilliant strategy is

00:39:42.266 --> 00:39:45.320
nothing more than words on a page.

00:39:47.280 --> 00:39:49.246
It can be hard for

00:39:49.246 --> 00:39:53.179
many smart ambitious people like

00:39:53.179 --> 00:39:56.550
you to accept the unexpected

00:39:56.550 --> 00:40:00.070
virtues of grunt [LAUGH] work.

00:40:01.630 --> 00:40:04.326
My third learning in life.

00:40:07.255 --> 00:40:11.136
To paraphrase the great American

00:40:11.136 --> 00:40:14.602
humorist P.J. O'Rourke,

00:40:14.602 --> 00:40:18.760
everybody wants to save the world,

00:40:18.760 --> 00:40:22.372
nobody wants to do the dishes.

00:40:22.372 --> 00:40:25.970
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; If you want to change

00:40:25.970 --> 00:40:29.585
lives, change organizations,

00:40:29.585 --> 00:40:33.198
and change the world, you need to

00:40:33.198 --> 00:40:35.920
do the dishes every day.

00:40:37.530 --> 00:40:39.858
To accomplish really big things,

00:40:39.858 --> 00:40:42.105
you first have to accomplish a lot

00:40:42.105 --> 00:40:43.800
of really small things.

00:40:45.410 --> 00:40:47.314
Most of those small things may be

00:40:47.314 --> 00:40:48.730
tedious or thankless.

00:40:50.090 --> 00:40:52.425
Even supposedly glamorous jobs

00:40:52.425 --> 00:40:54.850
are usually not that glamorous.

00:40:56.260 --> 00:41:00.102
Your role will be filled with grunt

00:41:00.102 --> 00:41:02.490
work and execution.

00:41:04.260 --> 00:41:07.654
This applies to Silicon Valley,

00:41:07.654 --> 00:41:09.229
Wall Street and

00:41:09.229 --> 00:41:11.780
every place in between.

00:41:15.100 --> 00:41:17.144
Let me now return to where your GSB

00:41:17.144 --> 00:41:18.750
experience first began.

00:41:20.070 --> 00:41:22.499
The admissions application essay.

00:41:25.233 --> 00:41:26.995
I hope this does not evoke bad

00:41:26.995 --> 00:41:27.735
memories.

00:41:27.735 --> 00:41:31.706
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; At the time,

00:41:31.706 --> 00:41:34.739
you had to explain what matters

00:41:34.739 --> 00:41:36.728
most to you and why.

00:41:36.728 --> 00:41:38.898
The point of the exercise was

00:41:38.898 --> 00:41:41.250
to bring out the authentic self

00:41:41.250 --> 00:41:41.890
in you.

00:41:43.510 --> 00:41:45.455
Stanford was not looking for

00:41:45.455 --> 00:41:47.401
a grandiose statement about

00:41:47.401 --> 00:41:49.427
the destiny of humankind, or

00:41:49.427 --> 00:41:51.939
radical solutions to climate change

00:41:51.939 --> 00:41:53.884
and sustainable growth, or

00:41:53.884 --> 00:41:56.490
the next technological revolution.

00:41:58.580 --> 00:42:01.743
Instead, they wanted to know what

00:42:01.743 --> 00:42:04.150
gives your life meaning.

00:42:06.260 --> 00:42:10.021
Not somebody else's life,

00:42:10.021 --> 00:42:11.591
your life.

00:42:11.591 --> 00:42:14.903
The GSB wanted you to focus on what

00:42:14.903 --> 00:42:17.278
really matters to you.

00:42:17.278 --> 00:42:20.141
They wanted to know what the

00:42:20.141 --> 00:42:23.879
purpose and vision of your life is.

00:42:25.940 --> 00:42:28.921
Whatever path you choose,

00:42:28.921 --> 00:42:31.902
do not let ambition stand in

00:42:31.902 --> 00:42:34.631
the way of your purpose.

00:42:38.372 --> 00:42:41.388
Each of you has a different set

00:42:41.388 --> 00:42:43.077
of priorities,

00:42:43.077 --> 00:42:46.213
a different set of skills, and

00:42:46.213 --> 00:42:49.361
a different sense of purpose.

00:42:49.361 --> 00:42:51.896
Your ultimate goal should be to

00:42:51.896 --> 00:42:53.066
bring all of it

00:42:53.066 --> 00:42:55.120
into proper alignment.

00:42:57.580 --> 00:43:01.284
To achieve great feats in life,

00:43:01.284 --> 00:43:04.303
it is not enough to develop

00:43:04.303 --> 00:43:06.500
separate skills,

00:43:06.500 --> 00:43:10.481
you also have to discover the magic

00:43:10.481 --> 00:43:13.776
of aligning those skills and

00:43:13.776 --> 00:43:18.031
connecting them with your purpose,

00:43:18.031 --> 00:43:21.210
your vision of the future.

00:43:23.060 --> 00:43:26.012
That alignment of skills and

00:43:26.012 --> 00:43:28.841
that connection to purpose

00:43:28.841 --> 00:43:31.547
represents my biggest and

00:43:31.547 --> 00:43:34.877
most important lesson in life.

00:43:36.905 --> 00:43:39.255
I have tried to apply all these

00:43:39.255 --> 00:43:41.794
learnings throughout my life.

00:43:41.794 --> 00:43:45.773
This has meant maintaining optimism

00:43:45.773 --> 00:43:47.955
about the future and

00:43:47.955 --> 00:43:50.779
gaining confidence that I

00:43:50.779 --> 00:43:55.130
will always find a path forward.

00:43:55.130 --> 00:43:58.734
I will always figure out the way

00:43:58.734 --> 00:44:02.753
using the lessons of my failures as

00:44:02.753 --> 00:44:06.357
building blocks for success,

00:44:06.357 --> 00:44:10.378
focusing on relentless execution

00:44:10.378 --> 00:44:13.440
and attention to detail.

00:44:15.642 --> 00:44:20.868
And finally, and most importantly,

00:44:20.868 --> 00:44:24.787
making the whole of my skills

00:44:24.787 --> 00:44:29.524
greater than the sum of their parts

00:44:29.524 --> 00:44:34.424
while connecting them to my purpose

00:44:34.424 --> 00:44:38.360
and my vision of the future.

00:44:38.360 --> 00:44:42.312
The outcome of this magic has been

00:44:42.312 --> 00:44:46.271
the tremendous success of MSCI.

00:44:49.010 --> 00:44:51.740
I recognize that everyone here

00:44:51.740 --> 00:44:53.736
wants to be a unicorn.

00:44:53.736 --> 00:44:55.791
Raise their hand, who doesn't?

00:44:58.102 --> 00:45:01.022
But before I conclude,

00:45:01.022 --> 00:45:04.968
[LAUGH] I guess a few hands there.

00:45:04.968 --> 00:45:07.160
But before I conclude,

00:45:07.160 --> 00:45:10.119
let me remind you that we all need

00:45:10.119 --> 00:45:12.540
a higher mission in life.

00:45:14.460 --> 00:45:17.771
There are 8 billion people on this

00:45:17.771 --> 00:45:21.309
planet, and GSB graduates are among

00:45:21.309 --> 00:45:24.060
the extremely privileged.

00:45:26.000 --> 00:45:29.172
You have a responsibility to

00:45:29.172 --> 00:45:33.010
the privilege bestowed upon you.

00:45:35.570 --> 00:45:39.507
Use it to change the world and

00:45:39.507 --> 00:45:42.630
live a purposes life.

00:45:44.620 --> 00:45:48.905
And yes, I mean, everything else,

00:45:48.905 --> 00:45:52.945
please remember to do the dishes.

00:45:52.945 --> 00:45:56.695
&gt;&gt; [LAUGH] &gt;&gt; God speed, thank you.

00:45:56.695 --> 00:46:01.825
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:46:01.825 --> 00:46:11.825
[APPLAUSE]

00:46:29.862 --> 00:46:32.414
&gt;&gt; It is now my great honor and

00:46:32.414 --> 00:46:35.664
pleasure to introduce the GSB PhD

00:46:35.664 --> 00:46:37.644
class of 2023.

00:46:37.644 --> 00:46:47.594
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:46:47.594 --> 00:46:49.917
&gt;&gt; Our PHD program is small and

00:46:49.917 --> 00:46:52.745
exclusive with an admission rate

00:46:52.745 --> 00:46:53.860
under 6%.

00:46:54.950 --> 00:46:57.160
These individuals came here as

00:46:57.160 --> 00:46:59.710
students dedicating themselves to

00:46:59.710 --> 00:47:02.175
two or three years of coursework,

00:47:02.175 --> 00:47:04.640
followed by an intensive research

00:47:04.640 --> 00:47:06.935
apprenticeship in their chosen

00:47:06.935 --> 00:47:08.370
fields.

00:47:08.370 --> 00:47:10.844
Having become true experts, they

00:47:10.844 --> 00:47:13.319
leave us as colleagues setting out

00:47:13.319 --> 00:47:15.792
on a path to create new knowledge,

00:47:15.792 --> 00:47:17.924
which we know will help change

00:47:17.924 --> 00:47:19.716
the world of business and

00:47:19.716 --> 00:47:21.790
management for the better.

00:47:23.490 --> 00:47:25.643
Today, we formally celebrate

00:47:25.643 --> 00:47:28.044
the transition of these folks from

00:47:28.044 --> 00:47:30.364
students to future intellectual

00:47:30.364 --> 00:47:32.765
leaders who will play an important

00:47:32.765 --> 00:47:35.167
role in shaping our understanding

00:47:35.167 --> 00:47:37.179
of business in management.

00:47:38.250 --> 00:47:40.950
After their advisors place the hood

00:47:40.950 --> 00:47:43.560
of the Stanford Graduate School of

00:47:43.560 --> 00:47:46.260
Business on them, they will join us

00:47:46.260 --> 00:47:48.690
on the stage as we welcome them as

00:47:48.690 --> 00:47:51.310
colleagues into our profession.

00:47:52.790 --> 00:47:55.016
Diane Lee, Assistant Dean of

00:47:55.016 --> 00:47:57.330
the PhD program, will now read

00:47:57.330 --> 00:48:00.001
their names and their dissertation

00:48:00.001 --> 00:48:01.880
advisor will hook them.

00:48:27.222 --> 00:48:29.367
&gt;&gt; Wen Jia Ba.

00:48:29.367 --> 00:48:31.485
Wen Jia's research explores how

00:48:31.485 --> 00:48:33.755
online platforms including online

00:48:33.755 --> 00:48:36.101
advertising and virtual assistants

00:48:36.101 --> 00:48:38.217
like Alexa or Siri can adapt their

00:48:38.217 --> 00:48:40.258
user behavior by using advanced

00:48:40.258 --> 00:48:42.224
tools like machine learning,

00:48:42.224 --> 00:48:44.283
optimization and game theory.

00:48:48.913 --> 00:48:55.587
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Martino Bunkyo,

00:48:55.587 --> 00:48:58.107
Martino investigates the impact of

00:48:58.107 --> 00:49:00.711
artificial intelligence on digital

00:49:00.711 --> 00:49:02.727
markets and whether AI could

00:49:02.727 --> 00:49:04.820
facilitate collusion.

00:49:04.820 --> 00:49:09.334
He designs new AI-aware digital

00:49:09.334 --> 00:49:10.794
markets.

00:49:10.794 --> 00:49:13.947
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Daniel Chen,

00:49:13.947 --> 00:49:15.904
Daniel's research shows how

00:49:15.904 --> 00:49:18.254
competing multi-sided platforms

00:49:18.254 --> 00:49:19.584
such as Facebook and

00:49:19.584 --> 00:49:21.462
Twitter can be embedded into

00:49:21.462 --> 00:49:24.300
a general equilibrium framework.

00:49:24.300 --> 00:49:25.769
He uses this model to analyze

00:49:25.769 --> 00:49:27.423
the impact of these platforms on

00:49:27.423 --> 00:49:28.649
the broader economy and

00:49:28.649 --> 00:49:30.364
develops insights into how these

00:49:30.364 --> 00:49:32.091
platforms should be regulated.

00:49:32.091 --> 00:49:41.410
[APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; George Jitta Guay.

00:49:41.410 --> 00:49:43.070
George's research uses modern

00:49:43.070 --> 00:49:44.975
causal inference methods to explore

00:49:44.975 --> 00:49:46.819
behavioral economics in real world

00:49:46.819 --> 00:49:47.830
settings.

00:49:47.830 --> 00:49:49.475
His work informs our understanding

00:49:49.475 --> 00:49:50.845
of how different policies and

00:49:50.845 --> 00:49:52.326
information designs can affect

00:49:52.326 --> 00:49:53.657
businesses and consumers.

00:49:53.657 --> 00:49:58.790
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Z Yang Kang.

00:49:59.790 --> 00:50:01.451
Z Yang's research focuses on

00:50:01.451 --> 00:50:03.578
market design, mechanism design and

00:50:03.578 --> 00:50:05.550
industrial organization.

00:50:05.550 --> 00:50:07.569
By applying a theoretical lens to

00:50:07.569 --> 00:50:09.516
policy design and evaluation,

00:50:09.516 --> 00:50:11.607
Z Yang's research helps to create

00:50:11.607 --> 00:50:13.481
economic systems that are more

00:50:13.481 --> 00:50:15.440
efficient and more equitable.

00:50:19.980 --> 00:50:26.171
[APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Rupali Kahl.

00:50:26.171 --> 00:50:27.562
Through a large-scale

00:50:27.562 --> 00:50:29.857
study conducted in Rwanda, Rupali's

00:50:29.857 --> 00:50:31.736
research focuses on developing

00:50:31.736 --> 00:50:33.613
small and medium firms by making

00:50:33.613 --> 00:50:35.699
them more customer-centric, and

00:50:35.699 --> 00:50:38.010
by adopting marketing technologies.

00:50:38.010 --> 00:50:39.960
Her research provides insights for

00:50:39.960 --> 00:50:41.585
policy designed to alleviate

00:50:41.585 --> 00:50:43.153
poverty and develop firms.

00:50:43.153 --> 00:50:48.598
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Douglas Laporte.

00:50:48.598 --> 00:50:53.541
Douglas' research is about how

00:50:53.541 --> 00:50:57.936
to improve machine learning

00:50:57.936 --> 00:51:03.437
methods to predict stock returns.

00:51:03.437 --> 00:51:06.960
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Sue Liu.

00:51:06.960 --> 00:51:08.850
Sue's research explores stock

00:51:08.850 --> 00:51:11.020
market reactions to monetary shocks

00:51:11.020 --> 00:51:12.840
through a novel demand-based

00:51:12.840 --> 00:51:13.950
mechanism.

00:51:13.950 --> 00:51:15.296
Her work unveils the crucial

00:51:15.296 --> 00:51:16.979
role of intermediaries' demand in

00:51:16.979 --> 00:51:18.663
monetary transmission through her

00:51:18.663 --> 00:51:20.066
preferences for target share

00:51:20.066 --> 00:51:21.479
between equities and bonds.

00:51:21.479 --> 00:51:27.380
[APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Lina Lukianseva.

00:51:27.380 --> 00:51:29.327
Lina's dissertation investigates

00:51:29.327 --> 00:51:30.833
the value of information and

00:51:30.833 --> 00:51:32.212
learning in the context of

00:51:32.212 --> 00:51:33.655
recommendation systems,

00:51:33.655 --> 00:51:35.674
oligopolies, and markets for data.

00:51:35.674 --> 00:51:41.051
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:51:41.051 --> 00:51:43.690
&gt;&gt; Shweita Maria Fasu.

00:51:43.690 --> 00:51:46.187
Shwita's research examines

00:51:46.187 --> 00:51:49.083
the psychological and behavioral

00:51:49.083 --> 00:51:52.279
consequences of technology mediated

00:51:52.279 --> 00:51:54.975
recommendations including how

00:51:54.975 --> 00:51:57.870
the personalization and modality

00:51:57.870 --> 00:52:00.878
of delivery influence consumers.

00:52:00.878 --> 00:52:03.653
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Abraham Oshotsky.

00:52:03.653 --> 00:52:05.963
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Abraham's research

00:52:05.963 --> 00:52:07.358
uses survey methods and

00:52:07.358 --> 00:52:09.173
computational linguistics to

00:52:09.173 --> 00:52:11.057
investigate the mechanisms and

00:52:11.057 --> 00:52:12.034
consequences of

00:52:12.034 --> 00:52:13.988
culturally determined behavior

00:52:13.988 --> 00:52:15.590
in the context of markets,

00:52:15.590 --> 00:52:17.979
organizations, and broader society.

00:52:17.979 --> 00:52:26.381
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:52:26.381 --> 00:52:28.870
&gt;&gt; Natasha Overmeyer.

00:52:28.870 --> 00:52:30.780
Natasha's research examines how

00:52:30.780 --> 00:52:32.559
entrepreneurs acquire funding

00:52:32.559 --> 00:52:34.467
through their vision of the future

00:52:34.467 --> 00:52:36.310
and how investors evaluate early

00:52:36.310 --> 00:52:37.710
stage startups.

00:52:37.710 --> 00:52:39.155
She studies how evaluative

00:52:39.155 --> 00:52:40.851
practices may shape the startup

00:52:40.851 --> 00:52:42.799
funding ecosystem with attention to

00:52:42.799 --> 00:52:44.378
inequality and misconduct.

00:52:44.378 --> 00:52:52.569
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Edo Specter.

00:52:52.569 --> 00:52:54.511
Edo studies the effect of CEOs

00:52:54.511 --> 00:52:56.688
early life environments on their

00:52:56.688 --> 00:52:58.787
propensity to commit financial

00:52:58.787 --> 00:53:00.316
fraud and- [SOUND] &gt;&gt; Engage

00:53:00.316 --> 00:53:03.410
in mergers and acquisitions.

00:53:03.410 --> 00:53:12.597
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Ulises Velazquez.

00:53:12.597 --> 00:53:14.449
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Ulises studies how

00:53:14.449 --> 00:53:16.005
the supply of credit during

00:53:16.005 --> 00:53:17.764
the times of crisis can greatly

00:53:17.764 --> 00:53:18.982
depends on the type of

00:53:18.982 --> 00:53:20.850
financial intermediary.

00:53:20.850 --> 00:53:23.052
He also examines how monetary

00:53:23.052 --> 00:53:25.432
policy affects the refinancing

00:53:25.432 --> 00:53:27.460
decisions of landlords and

00:53:27.460 --> 00:53:30.014
in turn how this affects the rental

00:53:30.014 --> 00:53:31.967
prices that tenants pay.

00:53:31.967 --> 00:53:35.030
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Paul Ficinanza.

00:53:35.030 --> 00:53:37.184
Paul's research develops tools from

00:53:37.184 --> 00:53:39.203
natural language processing, and

00:53:39.203 --> 00:53:41.020
deep learning to illuminate how

00:53:41.020 --> 00:53:42.972
social structures mold knowledge

00:53:42.972 --> 00:53:43.798
and culture.

00:53:43.798 --> 00:53:50.952
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE].

00:53:50.952 --> 00:53:53.110
&gt;&gt; Lulu Weng.

00:53:53.110 --> 00:53:55.079
Stores pay high fees to accept

00:53:55.079 --> 00:53:56.103
credit cards so

00:53:56.103 --> 00:53:58.080
that consumers get rewards.

00:53:58.080 --> 00:54:00.213
Lulu's research shows that this is

00:54:00.213 --> 00:54:01.492
bad and- [SOUND] &gt;&gt; That new

00:54:01.492 --> 00:54:03.350
payment apps like Affirm or

00:54:03.350 --> 00:54:05.010
PayPal can make it worse.

00:54:05.010 --> 00:54:14.314
[APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Jonathan Zeller.

00:54:14.314 --> 00:54:16.040
Jon studies the trade offs that

00:54:16.040 --> 00:54:17.968
govern how consumers make choices

00:54:17.968 --> 00:54:19.498
in digital environments,

00:54:19.498 --> 00:54:21.625
including subscription software and

00:54:21.625 --> 00:54:22.502
TV streaming.

00:54:22.502 --> 00:54:24.242
His work helps us understand

00:54:24.242 --> 00:54:26.055
the implications of a firm's

00:54:26.055 --> 00:54:28.014
decision such as when to release

00:54:28.014 --> 00:54:29.910
episodes of streaming TV shows

00:54:29.910 --> 00:54:38.174
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Charles Zhang.

00:54:38.174 --> 00:54:39.901
Charles studies the value of

00:54:39.901 --> 00:54:41.629
salespeople's efforts and

00:54:41.629 --> 00:54:43.573
how information about consumer

00:54:43.573 --> 00:54:45.589
behavior can be use to accurately

00:54:45.589 --> 00:54:47.822
measure the value of these efforts.

00:54:47.822 --> 00:54:54.910
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Mingxi Zhu.

00:54:54.910 --> 00:54:56.799
Mingxi's research focuses on

00:54:56.799 --> 00:54:59.270
developing large-scale optimization

00:54:59.270 --> 00:55:01.450
algorithms to facilitate managing

00:55:01.450 --> 00:55:02.829
vast amounts of data,

00:55:02.829 --> 00:55:04.718
making effective decisions,

00:55:04.718 --> 00:55:06.171
and assessing business

00:55:06.171 --> 00:55:08.223
strategies in a short time span.

00:55:08.223 --> 00:55:17.583
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:55:18.874 --> 00:55:25.037
[APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; Good afternoon,

00:55:25.037 --> 00:55:27.496
it is my pleasure as Director of

00:55:27.496 --> 00:55:29.673
the Stanford MSX program,

00:55:29.673 --> 00:55:32.418
to present this year's graduates

00:55:32.418 --> 00:55:35.068
who received the degree of Master

00:55:35.068 --> 00:55:37.261
of Science in Management.

00:55:37.261 --> 00:55:42.466
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE]

00:55:42.466 --> 00:55:44.316
&gt;&gt; The mission of the MSX program

00:55:44.316 --> 00:55:46.611
is to provide experienced leaders,

00:55:46.611 --> 00:55:48.461
people who have spent at least

00:55:48.461 --> 00:55:50.460
eight years in professional and

00:55:50.460 --> 00:55:52.756
management roles, the opportunity

00:55:52.756 --> 00:55:54.385
to prepare themselves for

00:55:54.385 --> 00:55:56.385
increasing senior positions in

00:55:56.385 --> 00:55:58.400
organizations they will lead.

00:55:59.620 --> 00:56:02.351
MSX students participate in a full

00:56:02.351 --> 00:56:04.989
time year long course of academic

00:56:04.989 --> 00:56:07.719
study, giving them an all too rare

00:56:07.719 --> 00:56:09.790
chance for reflection and

00:56:09.790 --> 00:56:12.331
growth in the midst of an already

00:56:12.331 --> 00:56:13.947
promising career.

00:56:13.947 --> 00:56:16.073
On behalf of the administration of

00:56:16.073 --> 00:56:17.027
the program and

00:56:17.027 --> 00:56:19.153
the faculty who have taught them,

00:56:19.153 --> 00:56:20.984
join me in wishing each of them

00:56:20.984 --> 00:56:22.892
great fulfillment and success

00:56:22.892 --> 00:56:25.036
in the next phase of their career.

00:56:25.036 --> 00:56:33.738
&gt;&gt; [APPLAUSE] &gt;&gt; In

00:56:33.738 --> 00:56:35.840
honor of the service of former

00:56:35.840 --> 00:56:37.941
GSB Dean Robert Joss, who was

00:56:37.941 --> 00:56:40.380
himself a student in the program,

00:56:40.380 --> 00:56:42.649
the top 10% of the MSX class,

00:56:42.649 --> 00:56:45.341
ranked by academic performance, are

00:56:45.341 --> 00:56:48.650
designated as Robert Joss Scholars.

00:56:48.650 --> 00:56:50.155
As we read the names of the

00:56:50.155 --> 00:56:52.380
graduates, we will also announce if

00:56:52.380 --> 00:56:54.535
they have achieved the distinction

00:56:54.535 --> 00:56:56.065
of Robert Joss Scholar.

00:56:56.065 --> 00:56:58.735
Margaret Hayes, Associate Dean of

00:56:58.735 --> 00:57:01.049
the MBA and MSX programs, will

00:57:01.049 --> 00:57:03.808
now read our graduates' names while

00:57:03.808 --> 00:57:06.486
Dean Levin presents the diplomas.

